WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.920
ARUN KUMAR: Good
afternoon, everyone.

00:00:02.920 --> 00:00:05.370
Welcome to the penultimate
session for the day.

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:09.570
In this talk, we'll focus on
security by default in Anthos.

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:12.600
I don't have a demo,
so I can't really

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:14.307
blame the Wi-Fi if
something goes wrong,

00:00:14.307 --> 00:00:15.390
but let's see how it goes.

00:00:15.390 --> 00:00:18.771
[LAUGHTER]

00:00:18.771 --> 00:00:20.410
So I am Arun Kumar.

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:21.700
I go by AK.

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:24.490
I'm a technical program
manager with Google Cloud,

00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:29.450
specifically for GKE
container and Anthos security.

00:00:29.450 --> 00:00:31.910
For an agenda for
this talk, we'll

00:00:31.910 --> 00:00:35.090
start with what are the driving
philosophy and principles

00:00:35.090 --> 00:00:36.380
behind Anthos security.

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:42.650
Then, we'll dig deeper on
some of the security features.

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:45.560
We'll then move on to
discussing some best practices

00:00:45.560 --> 00:00:48.080
from a security
perspective for Anthos.

00:00:48.080 --> 00:00:50.840
We'll finally talk about
shared responsibility

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:54.360
and how the responsibility
for securing

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:58.460
an account [? stands ?] between
the provider and the user.

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:03.940
Time permitting, finally,
we'll have a Q&amp;A.

00:01:03.940 --> 00:01:06.720
Let's jump right in.

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:09.320
What is the philosophy
that drives the security

00:01:09.320 --> 00:01:11.750
work behind Anthos?

00:01:11.750 --> 00:01:17.690
So we wanted security by default
to be the underlying theme.

00:01:17.690 --> 00:01:21.740
Users should not have to become
security experts themselves

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:24.800
in their quest to
modernize their apps.

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:28.910
Our users have sophisticated,
pretty advanced,

00:01:28.910 --> 00:01:33.090
and enterprise-grade
on-premise systems.

00:01:33.090 --> 00:01:35.480
Most of these are for valid
business reasons, compliance

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:38.330
and regulations being
among the chief of them.

00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:41.630
We want to meet
customers where they are.

00:01:41.630 --> 00:01:44.090
The best of other
security capabilities

00:01:44.090 --> 00:01:47.900
should be able to integrate
seamlessly with your existing

00:01:47.900 --> 00:01:49.590
infrastructure.

00:01:49.590 --> 00:01:52.400
Finally, at Google
and Google Cloud,

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:55.380
we take security very seriously.

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:58.610
We want our users to
benefit from our security

00:01:58.610 --> 00:02:00.560
culture, which has
been battle-tested

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:03.820
and [INAUDIBLE] over the years.

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:06.580
We also have a strong
presence in the [INAUDIBLE]

00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:08.748
and Kubernetes product
security committee.

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:18.920
We started by writing a
security white paper for Anthos

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:24.390
where we identified and talked
about major security challenges

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:27.480
that users face as they
migrate from traditional

00:02:27.480 --> 00:02:29.090
to modern apps.

00:02:29.090 --> 00:02:32.520
The first among them is about
enforcing consistent policies

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:33.960
across environments.

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:37.230
This could be across nodes,
across clusters, even

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:39.420
across multiple clouds.

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:43.560
Anthos helps you solve
this problem with ACM.

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:45.910
Moving on to the
second major challenge,

00:02:45.910 --> 00:02:49.230
it's a question of how
can you effectively

00:02:49.230 --> 00:02:51.210
secure your software
supply chain

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:55.920
in your organization and only
deploy trusted workloads?

00:02:55.920 --> 00:02:58.200
The last one talks
about securing

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:01.760
multi-tenant environments
across a shared platform.

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:04.560
Two and three we'll address
shortly in our talk.

00:03:04.560 --> 00:03:07.500
I also urge you to take a
look at our Anthos security

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:08.090
whitepaper.

00:03:08.090 --> 00:03:11.640
It makes for an
interesting reading.

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:13.150
Moving on.

00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:16.560
So our capabilities
are organized

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:21.200
across these five core security
design principles that we have.

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:24.390
The first of them is
infrastructure security,

00:03:24.390 --> 00:03:27.630
then supply chain security,
and runtime security.

00:03:27.630 --> 00:03:30.710
The fourth one is
configuration and policies.

00:03:30.710 --> 00:03:33.430
The last one is compliance
and certifications.

00:03:33.430 --> 00:03:36.330
We will only talk about the
first three in this talk.

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:38.610
We already discussed
configuration and policies

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:41.710
in the ACM talk in
detail in the morning.

00:03:41.710 --> 00:03:43.410
Compliance and
certifications, that's

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:46.030
a whole big topic
unto themselves,

00:03:46.030 --> 00:03:49.320
and they deserve more, they
require more than half an hour

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:52.710
by themselves.

00:03:52.710 --> 00:03:55.300
Moving on.

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:59.295
Let's start with a
feature deep dive

00:03:59.295 --> 00:04:01.170
and with infrastructure
and network security.

00:04:05.650 --> 00:04:08.890
So Workload Identity,
which is in beta,

00:04:08.890 --> 00:04:11.530
addresses the problem
of how you can

00:04:11.530 --> 00:04:16.240
access GCP services from
within your Kubernetes and GKE

00:04:16.240 --> 00:04:17.920
workloads.

00:04:17.920 --> 00:04:21.130
Earlier, users faced
an impossible trade-off

00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:23.800
between security and usability.

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:27.340
If you wanted to access
seamlessly GCP services

00:04:27.340 --> 00:04:30.280
and resources, you either
had to forego principles

00:04:30.280 --> 00:04:32.890
of least privilege,
or you had to deal

00:04:32.890 --> 00:04:36.700
with burdensome config,
rotating credentials, and so on.

00:04:36.700 --> 00:04:38.830
So with Workload
Identity, we built

00:04:38.830 --> 00:04:43.070
a completely new IAM primitive
called identity namespaces.

00:04:43.070 --> 00:04:47.080
So what it does is it takes
your Kubernetes service account

00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:51.250
identity and binds it with a
Google Cloud Service account

00:04:51.250 --> 00:04:52.090
identity.

00:04:52.090 --> 00:04:55.180
And once that happens,
workloads only

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:57.340
have minimum and
required permissions,

00:04:57.340 --> 00:04:58.840
and Google takes
care of managing

00:04:58.840 --> 00:05:01.680
all the credentials and identity
stuff on the other side.

00:05:04.850 --> 00:05:09.090
Moving on, node auto-upgrade.

00:05:09.090 --> 00:05:11.400
This feature helps
keep your nodes up

00:05:11.400 --> 00:05:14.130
to date with the most recent
and the stable version

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:15.870
of Kubernetes.

00:05:15.870 --> 00:05:17.910
This is particularly
important in the context

00:05:17.910 --> 00:05:19.920
of security patches, right?

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:23.280
So if there is a security
patch that has been released,

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:28.590
GKE automatically patches
all your nodes for you.

00:05:28.590 --> 00:05:31.140
This is available for
both COS and Ubuntu.

00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:33.020
You can also schedule
a maintenance interval

00:05:33.020 --> 00:05:36.380
to define when you want
these upgrades to happen.

00:05:39.240 --> 00:05:41.000
Shielded GKE nodes.

00:05:41.000 --> 00:05:44.840
This protects the node
bootstrapping process

00:05:44.840 --> 00:05:47.380
by anchoring with a vTPM.

00:05:47.380 --> 00:05:50.930
A TPM is a specialized chip
that holds the encryption

00:05:50.930 --> 00:05:52.310
keys in your machine.

00:05:52.310 --> 00:05:55.790
A vTPM does the same thing
for a virtual machine.

00:05:55.790 --> 00:05:58.850
When you use this feature, your
secrets and your credentials

00:05:58.850 --> 00:06:01.940
are isolated and
protected in this vTPM.

00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:05.030
Exfiltration becomes
super difficult,

00:06:05.030 --> 00:06:08.880
and node identity is always
verified to the cluster.

00:06:08.880 --> 00:06:12.060
So arbitrary nodes
can't steal credentials

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:15.110
and then enroll with them by
themselves to the cluster.

00:06:18.860 --> 00:06:21.350
Application layer
secrets encryption.

00:06:21.350 --> 00:06:24.780
We are launching this in
GA, I think, last week.

00:06:24.780 --> 00:06:25.880
Please try it out.

00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:29.920
With this feature, what you
do is you take your secrets

00:06:29.920 --> 00:06:34.570
and credentials, you
encrypt them locally on ETCD

00:06:34.570 --> 00:06:38.240
with a data encryption
key, or a DEK.

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:42.720
This DEK is then encrypted by
a key encryption key which you

00:06:42.720 --> 00:06:45.550
manage and stored in Cloud KMS.

00:06:45.550 --> 00:06:50.710
Cloud KMS, or Cloud
Key Management Service,

00:06:50.710 --> 00:06:54.640
manages these keys for you and
also acts as a Root of Trust

00:06:54.640 --> 00:06:58.180
upon which you can hold
all your credentials.

00:06:58.180 --> 00:07:00.700
I have a mandatory
call-out to make here.

00:07:00.700 --> 00:07:04.680
So by default, Kubernetes
secrets are plaintext.

00:07:04.680 --> 00:07:06.160
They are not encrypted, right?

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:10.690
What GKE does for you is always
and automatically, by default,

00:07:10.690 --> 00:07:12.040
encrypt all your secrets.

00:07:12.040 --> 00:07:14.610
All of them are stored
encrypted in disks,

00:07:14.610 --> 00:07:18.760
and GKE also monitors
access for them, and so on.

00:07:18.760 --> 00:07:21.610
This feature is just an
additional level of control

00:07:21.610 --> 00:07:24.790
that we provide you
for encryption, mostly

00:07:24.790 --> 00:07:28.000
for regulatory requirements, and
if you have sensitive workloads

00:07:28.000 --> 00:07:30.370
that you want to hold
credentials to revoke,

00:07:30.370 --> 00:07:31.390
and rotate, and so on.

00:07:35.330 --> 00:07:43.320
Honorable call-out, we also have
similar encryption mechanisms

00:07:43.320 --> 00:07:45.140
where you can
encrypt [INAUDIBLE]

00:07:45.140 --> 00:07:47.500
and disks, along with
keys that you manage

00:07:47.500 --> 00:07:48.800
and you store in Cloud KMS.

00:07:51.530 --> 00:07:55.090
Let's talk about a couple of
networking security features.

00:07:55.090 --> 00:07:59.350
So network policy is a native
Kubernetes feature, right?

00:07:59.350 --> 00:08:01.690
Most of you would
be [INAUDIBLE]..

00:08:01.690 --> 00:08:05.800
What it does is it helps you
manage pod-to-pod traffic.

00:08:05.800 --> 00:08:09.315
By default, any pod is visible
to all pods within the cluster,

00:08:09.315 --> 00:08:12.710
and IPs are updated
in the cluster.

00:08:12.710 --> 00:08:17.670
So this feature helps you
restrict pod-to-pod traffic.

00:08:17.670 --> 00:08:20.440
Let's move on to VPC
service controls.

00:08:20.440 --> 00:08:22.322
Do you want me to go
a little bit slower,

00:08:22.322 --> 00:08:23.280
or is the pacing right?

00:08:25.943 --> 00:08:27.110
OK, I'll take that as a yes.

00:08:27.110 --> 00:08:27.815
Thank you.

00:08:27.815 --> 00:08:31.560
So this is one of my
most favorite features.

00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:35.960
VPC Service Controls
enables you to configure

00:08:35.960 --> 00:08:40.600
security perimeters around your
GCP projects and resources.

00:08:40.600 --> 00:08:43.070
So you control the
movement of data

00:08:43.070 --> 00:08:45.455
both in and out
of your projects.

00:08:48.470 --> 00:08:51.020
When the comm lines are
secured, data infiltration,

00:08:51.020 --> 00:08:53.270
you're protected against
data exfiltration

00:08:53.270 --> 00:08:55.920
from your projects.

00:08:55.920 --> 00:08:59.090
All data and resource
accesses across boundaries

00:08:59.090 --> 00:09:01.320
are also secured.

00:09:01.320 --> 00:09:05.650
If your workloads span hybrid
environments, for example,

00:09:05.650 --> 00:09:08.550
[INAUDIBLE],, you can use
private Google access,

00:09:08.550 --> 00:09:11.700
along with VPC Service
Controls, to draw a perimeter

00:09:11.700 --> 00:09:14.885
around your hybrid environment.

00:09:17.620 --> 00:09:19.430
Moving on.

00:09:19.430 --> 00:09:22.250
So this slide
actually represents

00:09:22.250 --> 00:09:27.520
a timeline of the GKE
security team's efforts

00:09:27.520 --> 00:09:31.450
towards hardening the GKE
product itself over the years.

00:09:31.450 --> 00:09:33.580
So as you can see, it
started from July 2017,

00:09:33.580 --> 00:09:38.190
and we have until 1.15.

00:09:38.190 --> 00:09:42.750
Interestingly, as a couple
of tidbits, if you see--

00:09:42.750 --> 00:09:44.400
sorry.

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:48.990
So if you see one and two,
these are actual vulnerabilities

00:09:48.990 --> 00:09:53.910
with demonstrated exploits
that we secured in time.

00:09:53.910 --> 00:09:57.720
So this represents a
GKE hardening timeline.

00:09:57.720 --> 00:10:00.630
It's fair to expect that
efforts towards hardening

00:10:00.630 --> 00:10:03.450
our on-prem installations will
follow a similar trajectory

00:10:03.450 --> 00:10:05.280
and pattern.

00:10:05.280 --> 00:10:07.860
The blue dots represent
feature launches

00:10:07.860 --> 00:10:10.140
that we did over
the course of time

00:10:10.140 --> 00:10:13.200
that we are also talking
about in this talk.

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:16.800
The green dots are
implementations

00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:21.430
of default settings
that we've done

00:10:21.430 --> 00:10:23.400
to reflect best
practices that we

00:10:23.400 --> 00:10:27.210
have advocated in our hardening
guides, and blogs, and so on.

00:10:27.210 --> 00:10:32.237
So these are available
to you by default.

00:10:32.237 --> 00:10:34.820
I'll just pause a few seconds
if you want to absorb the slide,

00:10:34.820 --> 00:10:35.945
and then I'll move forward.

00:10:41.006 --> 00:10:41.506
OK.

00:10:44.440 --> 00:10:47.000
How do you secure your
software supply chain?

00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:55.260
This is from code, build, test,
package, deploy, and so on.

00:10:55.260 --> 00:10:58.740
You start by using
managed base images.

00:10:58.740 --> 00:11:02.340
Google builds and maintains
these base images,

00:11:02.340 --> 00:11:03.870
and scans them for
vulnerabilities,

00:11:03.870 --> 00:11:06.350
and automatically patches them.

00:11:06.350 --> 00:11:11.970
So if you use this, this is
available on GCP Marketplace

00:11:11.970 --> 00:11:13.240
to any GCP customer.

00:11:13.240 --> 00:11:15.380
It kind of offloads
the responsibility

00:11:15.380 --> 00:11:19.020
of using patched images
away from you to Google.

00:11:19.020 --> 00:11:20.940
This is battle-tested,
used by Google.

00:11:20.940 --> 00:11:22.872
We actually use
it for App Engine

00:11:22.872 --> 00:11:24.080
on our own internal products.

00:11:26.670 --> 00:11:29.110
Vulnerability scanning.

00:11:29.110 --> 00:11:31.360
Container Analysis
is the official name.

00:11:31.360 --> 00:11:34.210
This is a feature that
scans all your images

00:11:34.210 --> 00:11:39.040
in your private Google Container
Registry for any known CVE,

00:11:39.040 --> 00:11:41.730
Common Vulnerabilities
or Exposures,

00:11:41.730 --> 00:11:44.950
from the public databases.

00:11:44.950 --> 00:11:48.020
This works for Debian,
Ubuntu, an Alpine images.

00:11:48.020 --> 00:11:50.620
Once you use a
feature, the data--

00:11:50.620 --> 00:11:53.740
the report that you get
from container analysis,

00:11:53.740 --> 00:11:57.170
you can either use
it internally--

00:11:57.170 --> 00:11:58.720
if you have [INAUDIBLE]
by yourself,

00:11:58.720 --> 00:12:02.650
you can [INAUDIBLE] the images
that your internal teams use.

00:12:02.650 --> 00:12:05.230
But most importantly,
this lets you

00:12:05.230 --> 00:12:09.840
prevent those unpatched
images from being deployed

00:12:09.840 --> 00:12:13.020
using binary authorization.

00:12:13.020 --> 00:12:15.650
This is a super
important feature.

00:12:15.650 --> 00:12:19.310
What it does is it lets
you, as an organization,

00:12:19.310 --> 00:12:22.445
define deployment
policies, and it

00:12:22.445 --> 00:12:26.430
codifies the deployment policies
for an entire organization.

00:12:26.430 --> 00:12:28.340
You can define
custom requirements

00:12:28.340 --> 00:12:30.590
as signatures or
attestations, or you

00:12:30.590 --> 00:12:32.780
can whitelist
repositories from which

00:12:32.780 --> 00:12:36.020
you want images to be deployed.

00:12:36.020 --> 00:12:38.090
So unauthorized images,
images that either

00:12:38.090 --> 00:12:41.970
have a vulnerability, that
are too old to be valid,

00:12:41.970 --> 00:12:44.540
that [? didn't ?] match
up with the attestation,

00:12:44.540 --> 00:12:46.670
they are blocked
from being deployed

00:12:46.670 --> 00:12:48.930
to any of the installations
at the Kubernetes--

00:12:48.930 --> 00:12:55.000
at the GKE control plane using
admission controller for that.

00:12:55.000 --> 00:12:58.610
At each stage of your
CI/CD pipeline in Google,

00:12:58.610 --> 00:13:04.200
these attestations are
checked and re-annotated.

00:13:04.200 --> 00:13:07.924
Let's move on to
runtime security.

00:13:07.924 --> 00:13:12.910
So GKE Sandbox-- this is
a very important feature

00:13:12.910 --> 00:13:18.950
if you run sensitive
workloads or if your workloads

00:13:18.950 --> 00:13:21.740
have varying levels of
risk profiles, right?

00:13:21.740 --> 00:13:24.643
Earlier, historically,
the way you isolate,

00:13:24.643 --> 00:13:27.060
let's say, sensitive workloads
from, let's say, a staging,

00:13:27.060 --> 00:13:29.850
or a developer, or a testing
workload in your organization

00:13:29.850 --> 00:13:34.680
was either to stand up new
VMs or, in worst cases,

00:13:34.680 --> 00:13:39.060
even to create new clusters to
isolate your workloads, right?

00:13:39.060 --> 00:13:42.692
With GKE Sandbox, you
can achieve isolation

00:13:42.692 --> 00:13:43.400
on the same node.

00:13:43.400 --> 00:13:46.520
You can have multiple
pods at varying levels

00:13:46.520 --> 00:13:50.850
of sensitivity and risk
running on the same pod--

00:13:50.850 --> 00:13:53.960
sorry, running on the same node.

00:13:53.960 --> 00:13:58.288
The isolation is at kernel
level and at runtime.

00:13:58.288 --> 00:14:00.000
A plug in there--

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:02.490
my co-workers Tim
Allclair and Greg Castle,

00:14:02.490 --> 00:14:07.400
they are giving a deeper talk
on isolation, container escapes,

00:14:07.400 --> 00:14:10.090
and sandbox overall tomorrow.

00:14:10.090 --> 00:14:11.977
So if you're interested,
please go ahead

00:14:11.977 --> 00:14:13.060
and register for the talk.

00:14:16.540 --> 00:14:20.110
Finally, a brief call-out for
Cloud Security Command Center.

00:14:20.110 --> 00:14:22.770
This is the central place on
Google Cloud Platform where

00:14:22.770 --> 00:14:27.130
you can unify, analyze,
and view all the security

00:14:27.130 --> 00:14:30.290
data for your organization.

00:14:30.290 --> 00:14:33.370
We are also integrated with
five of the security partners.

00:14:33.370 --> 00:14:38.040
So if you use any of
these five partners

00:14:38.040 --> 00:14:40.560
to augment your
security capabilities,

00:14:40.560 --> 00:14:42.450
all of their tools are
completely integrated

00:14:42.450 --> 00:14:44.810
with CSCC, and the data
is surfaced to CSCC.

00:14:48.290 --> 00:14:53.275
So rounding up Anthos
security capabilities.

00:14:53.275 --> 00:14:54.775
We went over
infrastructure security

00:14:54.775 --> 00:14:56.980
in detail, supply
chain, and runtime.

00:14:56.980 --> 00:15:00.640
We didn't get a lot of time
to focus on network security.

00:15:00.640 --> 00:15:04.010
I highly urge you to look
at the documentation.

00:15:04.010 --> 00:15:05.920
They're pretty detailed.

00:15:05.920 --> 00:15:07.800
Honorable mention, Cloud
Armor [? protects ?]

00:15:07.800 --> 00:15:09.330
from DOS attacks.

00:15:09.330 --> 00:15:13.570
Istio Security-- we already
had Istio in the morning.

00:15:13.570 --> 00:15:17.270
IAP, I'll talk about it briefly.

00:15:17.270 --> 00:15:20.980
Master Authorized Networks
lets you accept only traffic

00:15:20.980 --> 00:15:24.040
from whitelisted IPs.

00:15:24.040 --> 00:15:26.230
I'll put it up for a few
seconds and then move on,

00:15:26.230 --> 00:15:27.610
if you want to absorb it in.

00:15:35.610 --> 00:15:37.120
Let's move on to best practices.

00:15:40.100 --> 00:15:43.450
The first one is
centralized logging, right?

00:15:43.450 --> 00:15:45.910
We recommend that you
follow the single pane

00:15:45.910 --> 00:15:49.110
of glass architecture
wherever practical.

00:15:49.110 --> 00:15:50.960
Of course, if you
have sensitive data,

00:15:50.960 --> 00:15:53.900
if you have auditable data that
you want logged separately,

00:15:53.900 --> 00:15:55.920
you're always going
to segregate it.

00:15:55.920 --> 00:16:01.240
But having all your monitoring
and logging data centralized

00:16:01.240 --> 00:16:04.360
is of immense value in the
case of security forensics,

00:16:04.360 --> 00:16:09.220
or even as a way for you to
analyze and consume your data.

00:16:09.220 --> 00:16:13.600
Anthos is well integrated
with Google Stackdriver

00:16:13.600 --> 00:16:14.350
that you can use.

00:16:14.350 --> 00:16:16.160
It has a single pane of glass.

00:16:16.160 --> 00:16:19.230
Stackdriver also integrates well
with Prometheus and Fluentd,

00:16:19.230 --> 00:16:22.330
if you're already using them.

00:16:22.330 --> 00:16:25.670
The second best practice is
about protecting endpoints.

00:16:25.670 --> 00:16:30.430
This, of course, deserves
a separate talk by itself.

00:16:30.430 --> 00:16:34.570
But the tl;dr, I would say, is
we would recommend using Cloud

00:16:34.570 --> 00:16:39.850
IAP, or Internet Aware
Proxy, that enables a more

00:16:39.850 --> 00:16:44.750
context-aware evaluation
of a user's identity

00:16:44.750 --> 00:16:47.890
or authentication.

00:16:47.890 --> 00:16:50.860
We also recommend using
Apigee for API management

00:16:50.860 --> 00:16:53.600
if you're operating
at that scale.

00:16:53.600 --> 00:16:55.610
Three and four we
already discussed.

00:16:55.610 --> 00:16:58.290
Three is about enforcing
network perimeter boundaries.

00:16:58.290 --> 00:17:00.080
This is super
critical, especially

00:17:00.080 --> 00:17:03.370
as your workloads
span multiple clouds,

00:17:03.370 --> 00:17:06.619
or between clouds and hybrid.

00:17:06.619 --> 00:17:10.300
Enforcing policies and
constraints consistently--

00:17:10.300 --> 00:17:13.349
we primarily do it using ACM.

00:17:13.349 --> 00:17:16.190
Of honorable mention is
Port Security Policies.

00:17:16.190 --> 00:17:18.829
If you're a hardcore
Kubernetes user,

00:17:18.829 --> 00:17:21.170
you must be well aware
of Port Security Policy.

00:17:21.170 --> 00:17:23.329
It lets you define the
next level and very

00:17:23.329 --> 00:17:28.970
low-level security
policies for authorization.

00:17:28.970 --> 00:17:31.950
They also support
gatekeeper and OPA policies

00:17:31.950 --> 00:17:34.040
via policy controller
via ACM that

00:17:34.040 --> 00:17:36.320
lets you custom
create constraints

00:17:36.320 --> 00:17:40.350
that you want to reflect
your business processes.

00:17:40.350 --> 00:17:43.350
The last one is about
isolating workloads

00:17:43.350 --> 00:17:45.930
in a multi-tenant environment.

00:17:45.930 --> 00:17:54.060
So Anthos offers features at
multiple levels and layers

00:17:54.060 --> 00:17:56.000
of isolation.

00:17:56.000 --> 00:17:58.000
Firewalls, they might share.

00:17:58.000 --> 00:18:00.090
So if you use GKE
private clusters,

00:18:00.090 --> 00:18:01.860
it gives you private IPs.

00:18:01.860 --> 00:18:04.110
Your cluster is hidden
from the internet.

00:18:04.110 --> 00:18:07.390
Access to master is restricted.

00:18:07.390 --> 00:18:11.130
GKE Namespace is a native
Kubernetes construct, again.

00:18:11.130 --> 00:18:15.210
It lets you slice
and dice your cluster

00:18:15.210 --> 00:18:17.940
to reflect your use cases.

00:18:17.940 --> 00:18:20.610
You can create a new
space per app, per user,

00:18:20.610 --> 00:18:22.650
per team, or per
sub-organization

00:18:22.650 --> 00:18:25.580
in your company, and so on.

00:18:25.580 --> 00:18:28.020
Network policies we briefly
touched upon before,

00:18:28.020 --> 00:18:30.780
but essentially, the
suite of network policies

00:18:30.780 --> 00:18:35.220
supported offer you
fine-grained layer 3 and layer 4

00:18:35.220 --> 00:18:37.560
network-level access.

00:18:37.560 --> 00:18:40.060
ASM, or Anthos Service
Mesh, on the other hand,

00:18:40.060 --> 00:18:44.410
provides you service-level
access, or at an L7 layer.

00:18:44.410 --> 00:18:47.820
Finally, we touched upon GKE
Sandbox, which offers you

00:18:47.820 --> 00:18:48.810
protection at runtime.

00:18:52.390 --> 00:18:57.300
So let's move on to the final
section, Shared Responsibility

00:18:57.300 --> 00:18:59.450
Model.

00:18:59.450 --> 00:19:03.770
So security in the cloud
is a shared responsibility

00:19:03.770 --> 00:19:06.740
between the cloud
provider and the customer

00:19:06.740 --> 00:19:08.630
or the user, right?

00:19:08.630 --> 00:19:12.590
Broadly speaking, as we move up
the stack from infrastructure

00:19:12.590 --> 00:19:16.960
as a service up to
PaaS and then to SaaS,

00:19:16.960 --> 00:19:19.970
the responsibility owned
by the cloud provider

00:19:19.970 --> 00:19:21.710
increases dramatically, right?

00:19:21.710 --> 00:19:24.220
And things become easier
for you to manage,

00:19:24.220 --> 00:19:26.000
and your responsibility
decreases

00:19:26.000 --> 00:19:27.650
as you go up the stack.

00:19:27.650 --> 00:19:31.340
So specifically speaking, if
you are using the infrastructure

00:19:31.340 --> 00:19:34.185
as a service from
a cloud provider,

00:19:34.185 --> 00:19:36.200
the provider is
responsible for securing

00:19:36.200 --> 00:19:39.620
the hardware, the physical
machines, the storage attached

00:19:39.620 --> 00:19:42.390
to it, as well as the network
through which you communicate,

00:19:42.390 --> 00:19:43.190
right?

00:19:43.190 --> 00:19:46.510
As you move up the path, you
get more things out of the box,

00:19:46.510 --> 00:19:47.360
right?

00:19:47.360 --> 00:19:50.380
The provider might most
likely give you an identity.

00:19:50.380 --> 00:19:52.560
That's a full blown
identity system.

00:19:52.560 --> 00:19:55.850
You'll get data and
access management tooling.

00:19:55.850 --> 00:19:58.250
You'll have Authn
and Authz constraints

00:19:58.250 --> 00:20:04.430
natively supported and a few
more features like that, right?

00:20:04.430 --> 00:20:08.815
If you move further up
towards the SaaS layer,

00:20:08.815 --> 00:20:10.940
that's a really cool
operating system there, right?

00:20:10.940 --> 00:20:13.670
Most of the stuff, by
default, is managed for you.

00:20:13.670 --> 00:20:18.440
So the provider manages
deployment, even application

00:20:18.440 --> 00:20:20.600
security, scalability,
reliability, uptime,

00:20:20.600 --> 00:20:24.430
load balancing-- the
whole suite of them.

00:20:24.430 --> 00:20:27.560
So GKE or Kubernetes,
as you might assume,

00:20:27.560 --> 00:20:29.060
falls in the PaaS layer.

00:20:29.060 --> 00:20:37.580
So what Google owns and is
responsible for is a GKE

00:20:37.580 --> 00:20:38.190
master, right?

00:20:38.190 --> 00:20:41.650
The master is completely
managed for you.

00:20:41.650 --> 00:20:44.730
So it is hardened, it is
secured, it is protected,

00:20:44.730 --> 00:20:48.400
and it is upgraded
by GKE transparently

00:20:48.400 --> 00:20:50.250
and completely invisibly to you.

00:20:50.250 --> 00:20:53.760
You are responsible, A,
for maintaining the nodes

00:20:53.760 --> 00:20:55.800
that you rent, the worker
nodes where you have

00:20:55.800 --> 00:20:57.540
deployed your workloads, right?

00:20:57.540 --> 00:21:00.570
But even here, if you
use managed base images

00:21:00.570 --> 00:21:04.570
that we alluded to
a few slides back,

00:21:04.570 --> 00:21:08.680
and if you opt-in for
auto-upgrade, even

00:21:08.680 --> 00:21:10.820
this is completely
offloaded to Google.

00:21:10.820 --> 00:21:13.390
Google takes care of
maintaining these nodes for you.

00:21:13.390 --> 00:21:15.350
It's completely
out of your hands.

00:21:15.350 --> 00:21:18.160
So what you're essentially
left with in GKE

00:21:18.160 --> 00:21:21.910
is a responsibility for
securing your application--

00:21:21.910 --> 00:21:24.490
your application, your
dependencies, and whatever

00:21:24.490 --> 00:21:25.830
configuration you have.

00:21:25.830 --> 00:21:28.820
And if you change any of the
default config and settings,

00:21:28.820 --> 00:21:33.070
and then you're responsible
for securing the new surface

00:21:33.070 --> 00:21:36.970
that these updated
configs open up.

00:21:36.970 --> 00:21:40.950
So that's about GKE On
GCP shared responsibility.

00:21:40.950 --> 00:21:42.460
When you move to on-prem--

00:21:42.460 --> 00:21:44.860
and this is applicable for
certain other components

00:21:44.860 --> 00:21:46.360
of Anthos, also--

00:21:46.360 --> 00:21:48.940
as you can see, the shared
responsibility model

00:21:48.940 --> 00:21:51.050
changes dramatically, right?

00:21:51.050 --> 00:21:53.980
You as a user are responsible
for a lot of stuff.

00:21:53.980 --> 00:21:58.030
So while this means that you
have a lot more to contend

00:21:58.030 --> 00:22:00.370
with in terms of
management, this also

00:22:00.370 --> 00:22:04.240
means that you have more
control and more visibility

00:22:04.240 --> 00:22:08.090
over how you want to
define your workloads.

00:22:08.090 --> 00:22:11.980
So depending on your compliance,
and your regulations,

00:22:11.980 --> 00:22:14.050
and your existing
business infrastructure,

00:22:14.050 --> 00:22:16.420
I think some of these
pieces will move and change,

00:22:16.420 --> 00:22:18.840
but this is broadly true.

